 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1790). Words follow the Peters engraving's spelling and punctuation (pò!, un schiavo, lasciavi, scordiamci, nè, and Ferrando's and Guglielmo's echoes “tace,” and “in pace,” without the libretto's ellipses). Ferrando and Guglielmo sing the same words together, and so do Despina and Don Alfonso in the Presto; each singer has his or her own phrases. Despina's “Quello ch'è stato è stato” (bars 52–59) is the quartet's short recitative (Rezit.), then a tempo. IPA, word meanings and translations come from the app's reviewed Così libretto drafts (L45 and L46, IPA checked against Castel). IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
